(Phase 1: synthetic, Phase 2: customer  data)\n- Inference latency: ~10ms per sequence (acceptable)\n- Requires GPU for real-time multi-customer deployment  (Phase 2)\n- Better anomaly detection but higher complexity than simple autoencoder\n\n## Alternatives Reconsidered\n- Isolation Forest added as secondary anomaly detector (Phase 2)\n- LSTMs for direct forecasting (alternative, deferred)\n ```\n\n**Content for `adr2.md`**\n```markdown\n# ADR-002: Synthetic Data Generation\n\n## Status\nACCEPTED\n\n## Context\nPhase 1 MVP requires  synthetic time-series data mimicking greenhouse sensor behavior for training. We need realistic  data.\n\n## Problem\nHow to generate realistic synthetic data for anom aly detection model training?\n\nOptions considered:\n1. **Gaussian Noise**:  Simple, unrealistic\n2. **TimeGAN**: Realistic, but complex\n3. **Modified TimeGAN** (chosen):  Reduced complexity, preserves correlations\n\n## Decision\nUse  a TimeGAN-inspired architecture (modified) with reduced complexity to preserve correlations:\n- Architecture:\n  - Generative LSTM (hidden dim: 64)\n  - Discriminator LSTM (hidden dim: 64)\n  -  Regularized to ensure realistic correlation structure\n- Dataset:\n  - 1000 sequences\n  - 20 timesteps\n  - 4 sensors (temperature, humidity, CO2, light)\n\n## Rationale\n1.  **Realistic**: Maintains correlations between sensor readings\n2. **Efficient**: Lower complexity than full TimeGAN,  faster training.\n3.  **Controlled**: Allows control of data distributions\n4.  **Flexible**: Can generate diverse  scenarios\n5. **No External Data Needed**: Self-contained\n6. **Scalable**: Data generator scales to more sensors.\n\n## Consequences\n- Requires careful regularization to maintain correlation structure.\n- Not  as photorealistic as full TimeGAN\n- Needs to be validated against real data in Phase 2\n\n## Alternatives Reconsidered\n-  Pure Gaussian noise rejected for unrealistic behavior\n- TimeGAN too slow, complex for this phase\n ```\n\n**Content for `adr3.md`**\n\n```markdown\n# ADR-003: Modular Phase 1.5 Architecture\n\n## Status\nACCEPTED\n\n## Context\nPhase  1.5 requires scaling to multi-greenhouse, multi-customer support. Architecture must accommodate these.\n\n## Problem\nHow  to design a modular architecture for seamless scaling to multiple greenhouses and customers?\n\nOptions considered:\n1.  **Monolithic Architecture**: Not scalable, hard to maintain\n2. **Microservices**: Scalable, complex for a small team, harder to deploy\n3. **Modular Monolith** (chosen): Logical modules, good balance  of scalability and complexity\n\n## Decision\nUse modular monolith with key features:\n- Core Module:  Anomaly detection\n- Greenhouse Module: Separate module per greenhouse (sensor data, configuration)\n-  Customer Module: Configuration, permissions, billing\n- REST API layer\n- Database layer:  PostgreSQL (extendable for multi-tenancy).\n\n## Rationale\n1.  **Scalable**: Modules enable  independent scaling and deployment\n2. **Manageable**: Monolith keeps complexity low for this phase\n3.  **Maintainable**: Modules separate concerns, enable independent updates\n4.  **Flexible**: Can transition to microservices later\n5.  **Cost Effective**:  Lower operational overhead.\n\n## Consequences\n- Requires clear module boundaries\n- Potential for  dependency issues between modules\n- Requires API design for modularity (internal and external)\n- Database schema needs to support  multi-tenancy.\n\n## Alternatives Reconsidered\n- Monolithic architecture is not scalable\n- Microservices add excessive complexity\n```\n\nOkay, I have now extracted all the content. Now let's try to construct the `finish` call.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"phases\": [\n    {\n      \"phase\": 1,\n      \"parallel\": false,\n      \"agents\": [\n        {\n          \"agent\": \"architect\",\n          \"task\": \"Create the `ARCHITECTURE_ASSESSMENT.md` file based on the instructions in `/Users/eugenesidorov/.gemini/antigravity/brain/acac912509062