2026-06-08 18:32:28,177 - root - INFO - RAW WS MSG: {"seqNum":"12","timestampMicros":"1780939945228835","stepUpdate":{"cascadeId":"2060e988590ca7b582c12c398ed48492","trajectoryId":"2060e988590ca7b582c12c398ed48492","stepIndex":1,"state":"STATE_ACTIVE","source":"SOURCE_MODEL","target":"TARGET_USER","thinking":"**Defining Adapter's Scope**\n\nI'm currently focused on defining the scope of the Vendor Adapter design document for the AI Agronomist platform. The user wants the design at `docs/architecture/vendor_adapter_design.md`. Right now, I'm concentrating on the abstract vendor interface specification, ensuring a clear and concise outline.\n\n\n**Structuring the Document**\n\nI've outlined the core markdown document structure for the Vendor Adapter design. I'm focusing on sectioning the document with clear headings. I'm prioritizing the Abstract Vendor Interface Specification, defining all the necessary methods, input/output contracts, and error handling guidelines. Then, I will address Concrete Adapter Implementations for CSV and Priva, followed by the Data Normalization Layer, and finally, extensibility considerations.\n\n\n**Outlining Document Content**\n\nI'm now diving into the detailed content outline for the design document. I'm starting with the Abstract Vendor Interface, specifying methods like `connect()`, `read_data()`, etc. I'm meticulously detailing input/output contracts and common exceptions. Next, I plan to move on to the Data Normalization section, where I'll define the canonical data format aligned with the LSTM-VAE input. Then, I plan to map examples of vendor-specific to the canonical format.\n\n\n**Defining Adapter Strategy**\n\nI've sketched out the initial document structure and content. I will start with creating the file and drafting the title. Next, I'll flesh out the overview section, focusing on the purpose of the adapter layer, and the abstract vendor interface. I will then specify methods like `connect()`, `read_data()`, and `get_status()`, then detail input/output contracts, and define common exceptions.\n\n\n**Defining Adapter's Scope**\n\nI'm now drafting the document content, starting with a title and status, the overview, and then diving into the details. I am carefully outlining the purpose of the Vendor Adapter layer. I'm focusing on providing a unified interface for data access. I'll make sure it abstracts complexities. I am aiming to ensure flexibility and extensibility by normalizing data formats into a canonical schema.\n\n```markdown\n# Vendor Adapter Design\n\n**Document Status :** DRAFT\n**Author:** Architect\n\n## 1. Overview\n\nThe Vendor Adapter layer is a critical component of  the AI Agronomist platform, responsible for abstracting the complexities of data ingestion from various third-party greenhouse control systems and  sensors. Its primary purpose is to provide a unified interface for the rest of the application to access sensor data, regardless of the source  vendor, format, or protocol.\n\nThis decouples our core application logic from specific vendor implementations, allowing for greater flexibility and  extensibility. By normalizing disparate data formats into a single canonical schema, we ensure data consistency for downstream consumers like the LSTM \n```\n\n\n**Drafting Abstract Interface**\n\nI'm now drafting the content for the design document. I've begun defining the `AbstractVendorAdapter` interface in Python, specifying the `__init__`, `connect`, and other methods. I'm focusing on clearly defining input parameters (like a configuration dictionary) and potential exceptions. I'll make sure to specify input and output data types, and any applicable data types or formats.\n\n\n**Defining Interface Methods**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the `AbstractVendorAdapter` interface definition within the design document. I'm adding `connect()`, `read_data()`, and `disconnect()` methods with detailed descriptions and type hints. I'm also including `get_status()`, defining the expected return format for status information, and outlining potential exceptions like `ConnectionError` and `DataReadError`.\n\nI'm now drafting the design document, defining the `AbstractVendorAdapter` interface in Python, specifying the `__init__`, `connect`, and other methods. I'm focusing on clearly defining input parameters (like a configuration dictionary) and potential exceptions. I'll make sure to specify input and output data types, and any applicable data types or formats.\n\n\n**Refining Method Specifications**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the specifications for the methods defined within the `AbstractVendorAdapter` interface. I'm expanding the descriptions for each method, `connect()`, `read_data()`, and `disconnect()`, providing more context on their expected behaviors. I'm also ensuring that exception handling is clearly defined, and that the data types for input and output are correctly specified, including handling of timestamps. I'm also updating the status methods.\n\n\n**Refining Abstract Interface**\n\nI'm now revising the design document with a complete `AbstractVendorAdapter` definition, including method signatures and detailed descriptions. I've clearly defined expected input and output data types, along with common exceptions, and I'm ensuring comprehensive coverage. I'm focusing on ensuring it's a solid, functional and extensible foundation.\n\n\\n\\n\n**Defining CSV Adapter**\n\nI'm now starting to outline the `CSVAdapter` implementation within the design document. I'm detailing its configuration parameters (file path, column mapping), including error handling for file operations. I'm also describing the logic for how it reads data using Pandas. Finally, I'm documenting the normalization process to the canonical format.\n\n\n**Drafting CSV Implementation**\n\nI'm now detailing the implementation specifics of the `CSVAdapter`. I'm outlining how it will use the `pandas` library to read CSV data and the error-handling strategies for file