2026-06-08 18:34:18,135 - root - INFO - RAW WS MSG: {"seqNum":"143","timestampMicros":"1780940058135057","stepUpdate":{"cascadeId":"09e1e7e5f6cfc90ae119e80073306d88","trajectoryId":"09e1e7e5f6cfc90ae119e80073306d88","stepIndex":41,"state":"STATE_ACTIVE","source":"SOURCE_MODEL","target":"TARGET_USER","thinking":"**Refining Test Structure**\n\nI'm pleased that `conftest.py` is lean; no database or API fixtures are cluttering it up. I'm building a focused test suite for `tests/api/test_main.py`. However, I'm now considering asynchronous testing with FastAPI. I will probably integrate `asyncio` or `httpx` to facilitate this process.\n\n\n**Evaluating Test Strategies**\n\nI've decided to proceed with the synchronous `TestClient` for my tests. It seems perfectly suited for FastAPI, and elegantly handles startup/shutdown event testing, even when using async lifespan hooks! I'm planning a comprehensive `tests/api/test_main.py` that will cover app startup/shutdown, CORS origin checks (targeting `http://localhost:8501`), `/health` endpoint response verification (including headers), and dependency overrides for `/health/db`.\n\n\n**Designing Test Cases**\n\nMy focus is now on structuring `tests/api/test_main.py`. I want a test suite that's easily readable, and requires minimal dependencies, but achieves 100% test coverage for `main.py`. I'm planning to mock `AsyncSession` and `execute` for SQLAlchemy 2.0 using a clean, standardized setup. I'll test successful SQL connections and also the behavior of the application when database connection fails. I also want to verify the `X-Request-ID` and various exception-handling scenarios.\n\n\n**Implementing API Tests**\n\nI'm now writing several specific tests in `tests/api/test_main.py`. I'm verifying the `/health` endpoint responds with a 200 OK, checks that the returned JSON data contains expected keys like \"status,\" \"app,\" and \"timestamp,\" and confirms the \"X-Request-ID\" header is present and the \"X-Process-Time-Ms\" header is also included. I'm also confirming correct CORS headers are returned for a valid origin and testing Request ID propagation, including checking that a custom ID is successfully passed through. These tests use `TestClient` and `pytest.mark.asyncio`.\n\n\n**Verifying DB Health Checks**\n\nI'm currently working to implement tests for database health checks. I'm focusing on mocking `AsyncSession` to simulate both successful and failed database connections. I'm using `AsyncMock` to mimic the database interactions and test different scenarios with `TestClient`. I'm verifying the HTTP status codes, responses, and dependency overrides to ensure robustness.\n\n\n**Testing Exception Handling**\n\nNow I'm focusing on exception handling within `tests/api/test_main.py`. I've added a test to verify the handling of 503 errors and database connection failures. I'm also confirming correct handling of 404 Not Found errors using a `GET` request on a nonexistent endpoint. Next I'll start testing and verifying validation error exceptions. I will either define a test route or mock `RequestValidationError`.\n\n\n**Implementing Validation Tests**\n\nI'm now implementing validation error testing. I've designed a test route within `tests/api/test_main.py` to raise a `RequestValidationError`, allowing me to confirm my custom error handler's functionality. I've also added a dummy route to trigger and test the 500 Internal Server Error handler, which enables complete coverage. These are amazing test cases!\n\n\n","textDelta":"","thinkingDelta":"**Implementing Validation Tests**\n\nI'm now implementing validation error testing. I've designed a test route within `tests/api/test_main.py` to raise a `RequestValidationError`, allowing me to confirm my custom error handler's functionality. I've also added a dummy route to trigger and test the 500 Internal Server Error handler, which enables complete coverage. These are amazing test cases!\n\n\n","text":""}}
