2026-06-09 14:50:25,988 - root - INFO - RAW WS MSG: {"seqNum":"2","timestampMicros":"1781013025987697","stepUpdate":{"cascadeId":"8f5f46ce496d34fccd7882e671b45cbe","trajectoryId":"8f5f46ce496d34fccd7882e671b45cbe","stepIndex":0,"state":"STATE_DONE","source":"SOURCE_USER","target":"TARGET_MODEL","thinking":"","textDelta":"<agent_context>\nSecurity, quality, and guardrails audit. Read files and provide report/ADRs only. No edits to source code.\n\nSPECIFIC REVIEW RESPONSIBILITIES:\n1. Verify guardrails compliance and check for performance, security, and correctness.\n2. File ADR-001 (architecture), ADR-002 (synthetic data), and ADR-003 (modular architecture) under docs/team/ADRs/.\n3. Create docs/review/week1-review.md assessment.\n\nGUARDRAILS:\n# Universal Guardrails\n\n## STOP WORK — never proceed if found\n- Tight coupling between LSTM-VAE and API layer\n- Secrets or API keys hardcoded in source files\n- Model checkpoint saved without metadata (epoch, loss, date, config)\n- Database schema without tenant_id field\n- Test coverage below 80% on critical paths\n- Major design change without ADR in docs/architecture/\n\n## WARN — log and continue\n- Hard-coded thresholds (should be in src/config.py)\n- Missing error handling on external calls\n- Code duplication (3+ identical blocks)\n- Silent failures (bare except, pass)\n\nRED FLAGS:\n# Red Flags — Immediate STOP WORK\n\nIf you see any of these, stop immediately and report to the orchestrator:\n\n1. Any file writing to a path outside its ownership (see CLAUDE.md file ownership table)\n2. Any import of os.system, subprocess with shell=True, or eval()\n3. Any hardcoded password, token, or API key\n4. Any DELETE or DROP in database queries without tenant_id filter\n5. Any model.save() without accompanying metadata JSON\n6. Test file that imports from and modifies implementation files\n7. Circular imports between src/models/ and src/api/\n\n### ACTIVE HANDOVER EXPECTATIONS (WEEK1-FOUNDATION-HANDOVER)\n# Week 1-2 Foundation Handover\nhandover_id: \"week1-foundation\"\nname: \"Week 1-2 Foundation Handover\"\ndescription: \"ML + Backend + DevOps collaborate on foundation\"\n\ncontext: |\n  Week 1-2: Foundation Phase\n\n  ML Engineer is building:\n  - LSTM-VAE model\n  - Synthetic data generator\n  - Training pipeline\n\n  Backend Engineer needs:\n  - Model input/output specifications\n  - Data shape and format\n\n  DevOps Lead needs:\n  - Python dependencies\n  - Memory/compute requirements\n\n  Architect reviews:\n  - Model architecture\n  - Data schema\n  - Integration points\n\ndependencies:\n  - ML provides: Model input/output specs\n  - Backend provides: Schema design\n  - DevOps provides: Compute requirements\n\nhandover_points:\n  day_2:\n    from: \"ml-engineer\"\n    to: \"backend-engineer\"\n    content: \"Model input/output specs\"\n\n  day_3:\n    from: \"backend-engineer\"\n    to: \"devops-lead\"\n    content: \"Python dependencies list\"\n\n  day_4:\n    from: \"all\"\n    to: \"architect\"\n    content: \"Design review and ADRs\"\n</agent_context>\n\n<context_from_previous_agents>\n=== backend-engineer output ===\n# Backend Engineering Code Review: FastAPI Ingestion & Inference Layers\n\nThis document presents a comprehensive review of the FastAPI route logic in [inference.py](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/inference.py) and [upload.py](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/upload.py). The review evaluates these routes against robustness, error handling, performance benchmarks, memory leak hazards, and architectural alignment as defined in the Week 2 Backend Guidelines and project constraints.\n\n---\n\n## 🛑 Blockers (Immediate Action Required)\n\n### 1. Tight-Coupling of ML Post-Processing inside FastAPI Routes\n\nIn [inference.py:L50-51](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/inference.py#L50-L51) and [L156-157](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/inference.py#L156-L157), VAE model raw reconstruction error scaling and clipping are implemented directly within the routing layer:\n\n```python\nraw_scores = detector.predict_batch(...)\nbatch_scores = np.clip(raw_scores / 2.0, 0.0, 1.0)\n```\n\n- **The Issue:** The dividing divisor (`2.0`) and clipping range (`0.0` to `1.0`) are mathematical post-processing heuristics. Hardcoding them in the API tier violates the **Loose Coupling** guardrail. If model configuration parameters change (such as hidden dimension scales or the beta divergence ratio), the reconstruction scores scale shifts, and the backend routes must be modified to prevent incorrect scaling or clipping.\n- **Consequence:** Structural coupling breaks modularity between the ML model and the delivery API.\n- **📝 Recommendation:** Move this scoring heuristic inside `AnomalyDetector.predict_batch` or a model post-processor class so the route layer only deals with finalized, scaled `[0..1]` scores.\n\n### 2. Unbounded Memory Leak in Model Caching (`_model_cache`)\n\nIn [inference.py:L12](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/models/inference.py#L12) and [L35-46](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/models/inference.py#L35-L46), loaded VAE models are cached indefinitely in a class-level dictionary:\n\n```python\n_model_cache: Dict[str, LSTMVAE] = {}\n```\n\n- **The Issue:** There is no cache eviction strategy (like Least-Recently-Used or TTL pruning). Over time, in a multi-tenant setup where dynamically-trained tenant-specific models (checkpoints) are reloaded across hundreds or thousands of clients, this cache will grow unbounded.\n- **Consequence:** This will cause a progressive memory leak, eventually triggering server-wide container Out Of Memory (OOM) fatal crashes.\n- **📝 Recommendation:** Integrate `cachetools.LRUCache` or a simple queue-based size constraint (e.g., maximum 10 models in memory, evicting the oldest) inside `AnomalyDetector`'s model loader.\n\n### 3. Blind Temporal Index Matching & Race Condition in Score Persist\n\nIn [inference.py:L58-76](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/inference.py#L58-L76), anomaly scores are matched and persisted back to the database purely based on sequence lists and index limits:\n\n```python\nstmt = select(SensorReading).where(\n    SensorReading.tenant_id == request.tenant_id,\n    SensorReading.greenhouse_id == request.greenhouse_id,\n).order_by(SensorReading.timestamp.desc()).limit(len(batch_scores))\n...\nfor idx, record in enumerate(reversed(records)):\n    if idx < len(batch_scores):\n        record.anomaly_score = float(batch_scores[idx])\n```\n\n- **The Issue:** Timestamps or unique reading identifiers of the incoming sequence array are not compared against the database records! The code assumes the latest database entries perfectly correspond to the array elements.\n- **Consequence:**\n  1. If a client transmits historical data or out-of-order sequences, wrong anomaly scores will be written to random database rows.\n  2. If the DB contains fewer records than the request batch size ($K < \\text{len}(scores)$), the database update fails silently for the trailing scores.\n  3. Under concurrent uploads, standard race conditions will result in database entries receiving completely mismatched scores.\n- **📝 Recommendation:** Expose specific record IDs or explicit timestamps in the `InferenceRequest` body, and utilize a targeted bulk update query with exact matches instead of sliding index assumptions.\n\n### 4. Excessive In-Memory Allocations during CSV Upload (Scale Memory Leak)\n\nIn [upload.py:L25-27](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/upload.py#L25-L27) and [inference.py:L110](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/inference.py#L110), file contents are read entirely into memory as raw bytes, decoded to unicode strings, and loaded as full Pandas DataFrames:\n\n```python\ncontent = await file.read()\ndf = pd.read_csv(StringIO(content.decode(\"utf-8\")))\n```\n\n- **The Issue:** Reading large uploaded bulk files directly into RAM creates a triple-copy allocation peak (raw bytes + decoded unicode string + String IO buffers + parsed DataFrame objects).\n- **Consequence:** If a vendor attempts a bulk historics upload of 50M-100MB, the server RAM usage spikes by 500M-1GB, causing massive latency bottlenecks or OOM aborts.\n- **📝 Recommendation:** Stream the file chunks to a temporary filesystem path (`tempfile`), or process the stream in batches using pandas' `chunksize` parameters. Ensure file unlinking in `inference.py` is safely wrapped in `try...finally` boundaries.\n\n### 5. RAM Spill via Thick SQLAlchemy ORM Object Instantiation\n\nIn [upload.py:L58-71](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/upload.py#L58-L71), bulk CSV records are iterated, individual Python objects are initialized, and tracked via session:\n\n```python\nrecords = []\nfor _, row in df.iterrows():\n    reading = SensorReading(...)\n    records.append(reading)\ndb.add_all(records)\n```\n\n- **The Issue:** Generating individual thick SQLAlchemy ORM instances for thousands of CSV readings forces the AsyncSession identity map to maintain full state tracking, dirty-checks, and session reference markers for thousands of Python objects.\n- **Consequence:** Memory spills inside the active thread and bulk inserts are exceptionally sluggish.\n- **📝 Recommendation:** Utilize standard SQLAlchemy 2.0 Core bulk inserts to completely bypass ORM state tracking overhead:\n  ```python\n  from sqlalchemy import insert\n  # Prepare a fast payload list of raw dicts\n  records_dict = df.to_dict(orient=\"records\")\n  await db.execute(insert(SensorReading), records_dict)\n  ```\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Warnings (Areas to Improve)\n\n### 1. Vendor Abstraction Layer Bypass & Severe Code Duplication\n\n- **The Issue:** The specialized [CSVAdapter](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/vendors/csv_adapter.py) engineering in Week 2 was specifically designed to handle CSV uploads, parse inputs, standardise column sets, validate types, and convert timezone-naive timestamps to timezone-aware UTC format. Yet, [upload.py](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/upload.py) completely bypasses `CSVAdapter`, manually duplicating Pandas CSV parsers, checking column subsets, and validating limits directly in the route file.\n- **Consequence:** Violates modular separation of concerns and creates duplicated parsing points. If the csv model schema layout changes, both the adapter and the endpoint routes must be manually updated.\n- **📝 Recommendation:** Refactor `/upload` in `upload.py` to ingest and process data directly through `CSVAdapter`.\n\n### 2. Timezone Loss on Insertion (Data Inconsistencies)\n\n- **The Issue:** The physical database column `SensorReading.timestamp` is designated as `DateTime(timezone=True)`. However, `/upload` parses dates with `pd.to_datetime(df['timestamp'])` which strips timezone markers, producing timezone-naive timestamps.\n- **Consequence:** SQLite silently accepts raw timezone-naive inserts. Over time, when queries convert readings or filter them, timezone discrepancies skew historical graphs by hours.\n- **📝 Recommendation:** Enforce UTC conversion. Relying on `CSVAdapter.get_data()` automatically enforces and converts all timestamps into pristine timezone-aware UTC structures.\n\n### 3. Hardcoded Physical Boundary Thresholds\n\n- **The Issue:** Physical limit bounds of numerical variables in `/upload` lines 40-47 are hard-coded directly as raw numeric literals `(0, 50)`, `(0, 100)`, `(0, 1000)`, `(0, 2000)` inside the route.\n- **Consequence:** This violates backend development instructions: _\"Hard-coded thresholds should be in src/config.py — WARN\"_.\n- **📝 Recommendation:** Centralize physical canonical boundaries into `src/config.py` as globally shared constants, importing them into schemas and validators alike, as follows:\n  ```python\n  CANONICAL_TEMP_LIMITS = (0.0, 50.0)\n  CANONICAL_HUMID_LIMITS = (0.0, 100.0)\n  CANONICAL_CO2_LIMITS = (0.0, 1000.0)\n  CANONICAL_LIGHT_LIMITS = (0.0, 2000.0)\n  ```\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Quality, Schema & Documentation Pass Audit\n\n- **Type Hints:** Both modules contain full python type hinting across functions, return payloads, and FastAPI dependency annotations (`AsyncSession = Depends(get_db)`).\n- **Docstrings:** High quality and complete function descriptors detailing input and schema specifications.\n- **Pydantic Validation (Ingress Layer):** The API schemas are correctly authored in **Pydantic v2** conventions. High-quality tensor validation rules in `InferenceRequest` successfully block mismatched matrices (`seq_len != 20` or `input_dim != 4`) early before reaching PyTorch space.\n- **DB Guardrail Compliance:** All schema operations query and filter by the mandatory multi-tenant field `tenant_id` consistently, aligning with strict security requirements.\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 Recommended Action Plan\n\n### Step 1: Encapsulate Scoring Scale to Resolve Tight-Coupling\n\nModify `AnomalyDetector.predict` or `predict_batch` inside [src/models/inference.py](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/models/inference.py) to integrate raw scaling and clipping internally:\n\n```python\n# Inside AnomalyDetector:\ndef predict_batch(self, sequences: List[np.ndarray]) -> np.ndarray:\n    stacked = np.stack(sequences)\n    raw_scores = self.predict(stacked)\n    # Scaled and clipped post-processing encapsulated safely inside the model wrapper\n    return np.clip(raw_scores / 2.0, 0.0, 1.0)\n```\n\nThis cleans up [inference.py:L51](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/inference.py#L51) and avoids machine learning heuristic exposure in the routing layer.\n\n### Step 2: Implement LRU Eviction in Model Cache\n\nImport and utilize `cachetools` or restrict size dynamically:\n\n```python\n# Inside AnomalyDetector:\ndef _load_model(self) -> LSTMVAE:\n    if len(self._model_cache) > 10:\n        # Evict oldest loaded model to limit RAM footprint\n        first_key = next(iter(self._model_cache))\n        self._model_cache.pop(first_key)\n    ...\n```\n\n### Step 3: Refactor CSV Upload route to use `CSVAdapter` and SQLAlchemy Core Bulk Ingest\n\nReplace manual pandas operations in `upload.py` with the robust `CSVAdapter` workflow:\n\n```python\n# Refactored upload.py snippet:\n@router.post(\"/upload\", response_model=UploadResponse)\nasync def upload_csv(\n    file: UploadFile = File(...),\n    tenant_id: str = Form(...),\n    greenhouse_id: str = Form(\"default\"),\n    db: AsyncSession = Depends(get_db)\n):\n    if not file.filename.endswith('.csv'):\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=400, detail=\"Only CSV files are supported\")\n\n    content = await file.read()\n\n    # Process through the specialized CSV Adapter safely\n    with tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile(delete=False, suffix=\".csv\") as tmp:\n        tmp.write(content)\n        tmp_path = tmp.name\n\n    try:\n        adapter = CSVAdapter({\"filepath\": tmp_path})\n        df = adapter.get_data() # Validates, UTC converts, and converts schemas cleanly!\n    except Exception as e:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=422, detail=f\"Data pipeline validation failed: {str(e)}\")\n    finally:\n        if os.path.exists(tmp_path):\n            os.unlink(tmp_path)\n\n    # Core bulk insert to bypass individual ORM object overhead\n    records_dict = []\n    for _, row in df.iterrows():\n        records_dict.append({\n            \"tenant_id\": tenant_id,\n            \"greenhouse_id\": greenhouse_id,\n            \"timestamp\": row['timestamp'].to_pydatetime(),\n            \"temperature\": row['temperature'],\n            \"humidity\": row['humidity'],\n            \"co2\": row['co2'],\n            \"light\": row['light']\n        })\n\n    from sqlalchemy import insert\n    try:\n        await db.execute(insert(SensorReading), records_dict)\n        await db.commit()\n    except Exception as e:\n        await db.rollback()\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=500, detail=f\"Database persistent error: {str(e)}\")\n```\n\n\n=== ml-engineer output ===\n# ML Code Review: `run_inference_from_csv`\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n\nThis review covers the machine learning preprocessing and inference logic within the `run_inference_from_csv` function in `src/api/routes/inference.py`. The function is responsible for parsing a user-uploaded CSV, preparing the data for our LSTM-VAE model, running anomaly detection, and returning the results.\n\nOverall, the implementation is a solid first version, but there are several critical areas that require improvement to ensure robustness, maintainability, and model performance. The most significant issues are the use of hardcoded magic numbers for score scaling and anomaly thresholding, which violate project guardrails and make the system brittle.\n\n## 2. Key Findings & Recommendations\n\n### 🛑 Blocker: Hardcoded Magic Numbers\n\n**Finding:** The code contains two hardcoded magic numbers that are critical to the ML logic:\n\n1.  `batch_scores = np.clip(raw_scores / 2.0, 0.0, 1.0)`: The score normalization divisor `2.0` is arbitrary and not based on any statistical property of the model's output. This can lead to incorrectly scaled scores if the reconstruction error distribution changes.\n2.  `anomalies_detected = int((batch_scores > 0.8).sum())`: The anomaly threshold `0.8` is hardcoded. This is a critical hyperparameter that should be configurable and tuned on a validation dataset.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n\n- **[BLOCKER]** Both the normalization divisor and the anomaly threshold _must_ be moved to `src/config.py`.\n- The normalization divisor should be replaced with a more robust scaling method. I recommend using the 99th percentile of the reconstruction error from the validation set, which should be stored as part of the model's metadata.\n- The anomaly threshold should be determined systematically (e.g., using F1-score optimization on a labeled validation set) and stored in `src/config.py`.\n\n### ⚠️ Warning: Inefficient Data Handling\n\n**Finding:** The current implementation reads the entire CSV file into memory and then writes it to a temporary file. This approach is not scalable and will fail with large files due to excessive memory consumption.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n\n- **[MEDIUM]** Refactor the data loading logic to use `pandas.read_csv` with the `chunksize` parameter. This will allow for processing the data in a memory-efficient streaming manner.\n\n### 📝 Recommendation: Improved Timestamp Mapping\n\n**Finding:** The logic for mapping anomaly scores back to timestamps (`row_idx = idx + seq_len - 1`) assumes the data is a continuous, sorted time series. This could lead to incorrect timestamp associations if the CSV contains gaps or is unsorted.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n\n- **[LOW]** Add a validation step to ensure the `timestamp` column is sorted before processing. If it's not sorted, either sort it or raise an informative error to the user. This will make the timestamp mapping more reliable.\n\n### 📝 Recommendation: Add Structured Logging\n\n**Finding:** The function lacks structured logging, which is essential for debugging and monitoring in a production environment.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n\n- **[LOW]** Integrate a logging library (e.g., `loguru` or the standard `logging` module) to record key events, such as:\n  - The number of rows and sequences processed.\n  - The model ID and checkpoint path used for inference.\n  - The number of anomalies detected.\n  - The execution time for different stages (data prep, inference).\n\n## 3. Code Review Checklist\n\n| Category       | Status                       | Comments                                                                                                  |\n| -------------- | ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Quality**    | ⚠️ **Warning**               | Code is clean, but hardcoded values violate the \"no magic numbers\" principle.                             |\n| **Testing**    | ❓ **Unknown**               | No unit tests were provided for this function. Tests are needed to verify the preprocessing logic.        |\n| **Docs**       | ✅ **Passes**                | The function has a clear docstring.                                                                       |\n| **Arch**       | ✅ **Passes**                | The function is well-structured and follows a clear separation of concerns.                               |\n| **Guardrails** | 🛑 **VIOLATION (STOP WORK)** | Hardcoded sensor ranges and ML-specific constants (threshold, scaling factor) violate project guardrails. |\n\n\n=== tech-lead output ===\n# Frontend Review: `src/dashboard/pages/5_upload_operations.py`\n\n## 1. User Experience (UX)\n\n### Positive Aspects\n\n- **Clear Layout**: The use of two tabs (`Upload & Train Model` and `Upload & Find Anomalies`) provides a clear and intuitive separation between the two primary user workflows.\n- **Good Feedback**: The UI provides good feedback during operations, using `st.spinner` to indicate processing and `st.success`, `st.error`, and `st.warning` messages to inform the user of the outcome.\n- **Interactive Visualizations**: The anomaly detection tab includes a Plotly chart, which is a great way to visualize the results and make them easier for the user to understand.\n\n### Recommendations for Improvement\n\n- **Input Validation**: The `tenant_id` and `greenhouse_id` text inputs lack validation. A user could potentially submit empty or invalid values, which might lead to backend errors.\n  - **Suggestion**: Implement basic client-side validation to ensure that these fields are not empty before the form is submitted.\n- **Hardcoded Anomaly Threshold**: The anomaly score chart has a hardcoded threshold of `0.8`. This value should ideally be fetched from the backend or a configuration file, as it might change.\n  - **Suggestion**: Retrieve the anomaly threshold from the API and use it to draw the threshold line on the chart.\n- **File Upload Constraints**: There are no constraints or feedback on the size or format of the uploaded CSV file. A very large file could cause the application to hang or crash.\n  - **Suggestion**: Add guidance on the expected CSV format and a reasonable file size limit.\n\n## 2. Structural Stability\n\n### Positive Aspects\n\n- **Readability**: The code is well-structured and easy to follow. The logic for each tab is self-contained.\n- **Error Handling**: The code includes `try...except` blocks to handle `requests.exceptions.RequestException`, which is good for catching API-related errors.\n\n### Recommendations for Improvement\n\n- **Graceful Error Handling**: The error messages, while present, could be more user-friendly. Displaying a raw exception message might not be very helpful to a non-technical user.\n  - **Suggestion**: Parse the error responses from the API and display a more meaningful error message to the user.\n- **Large File Uploads**: The current implementation reads the entire uploaded file into memory (`uploaded_file_train.getvalue()`). This can be a problem for very large files.\n  - **Suggestion**: For a more robust solution, consider streaming the file upload to the backend.\n\n## 3. API Integrations\n\n### Positive Aspects\n\n- **Clear API Endpoints**: The API endpoints being used are clearly defined and easy to understand.\n\n### Recommendations for Improvement\n\n- **Synchronous Training Request**: The training process is triggered by a single, synchronous `requests.post` call. For a long-running process like model training, this is not ideal. The request could time out, and the user has no way of knowing the status of the training process once it has started.\n  - **Suggestion**: The backend should expose an asynchronous endpoint for training. The frontend would initiate the training process and then poll a separate endpoint for status updates. This would provide a much better user experience.\n- **API URL Configuration**: The `API_URL` is imported from `src/config.py`. This is good, but for a production application, it would be better to have this configurable via environment variables.\n\n## Summary of Key Recommendations\n\n1. **Implement input validation** for `tenant_id` and `greenhouse_id`.\n2. **Move the hardcoded anomaly threshold** to a configurable value.\n3. **Implement an asynchronous training process** with status polling.\n4. **Improve error handling** to provide more user-friendly messages.\n5. **Consider streaming for large file uploads**.\n\n</context_from_previous_agents>\n\nTASK: Consolidate the findings from the backend-engineer, ml-engineer, and tech-lead into a final executive summary. Read the review documents (`be_review.md`, `ml_review.md`, `fe_review.md`) and synthesize them into a single, coherent report. The final report should provide a high-level overview of the issues and recommendations from all reviews.\n\nIMPORTANT: Write your complete output directly to the file: final_review_report.md\nUse the Write/Create file tools to write the content itself, not Python code that creates it.","thinkingDelta":"","text":"<agent_context>\nSecurity, quality, and guardrails audit. Read files and provide report/ADRs only. No edits to source code.\n\nSPECIFIC REVIEW RESPONSIBILITIES:\n1. Verify guardrails compliance and check for performance, security, and correctness.\n2. File ADR-001 (architecture), ADR-002 (synthetic data), and ADR-003 (modular architecture) under docs/team/ADRs/.\n3. Create docs/review/week1-review.md assessment.\n\nGUARDRAILS:\n# Universal Guardrails\n\n## STOP WORK — never proceed if found\n- Tight coupling between LSTM-VAE and API layer\n- Secrets or API keys hardcoded in source files\n- Model checkpoint saved without metadata (epoch, loss, date, config)\n- Database schema without tenant_id field\n- Test coverage below 80% on critical paths\n- Major design change without ADR in docs/architecture/\n\n## WARN — log and continue\n- Hard-coded thresholds (should be in src/config.py)\n- Missing error handling on external calls\n- Code duplication (3+ identical blocks)\n- Silent failures (bare except, pass)\n\nRED FLAGS:\n# Red Flags — Immediate STOP WORK\n\nIf you see any of these, stop immediately and report to the orchestrator:\n\n1. Any file writing to a path outside its ownership (see CLAUDE.md file ownership table)\n2. Any import of os.system, subprocess with shell=True, or eval()\n3. Any hardcoded password, token, or API key\n4. Any DELETE or DROP in database queries without tenant_id filter\n5. Any model.save() without accompanying metadata JSON\n6. Test file that imports from and modifies implementation files\n7. Circular imports between src/models/ and src/api/\n\n### ACTIVE HANDOVER EXPECTATIONS (WEEK1-FOUNDATION-HANDOVER)\n# Week 1-2 Foundation Handover\nhandover_id: \"week1-foundation\"\nname: \"Week 1-2 Foundation Handover\"\ndescription: \"ML + Backend + DevOps collaborate on foundation\"\n\ncontext: |\n  Week 1-2: Foundation Phase\n\n  ML Engineer is building:\n  - LSTM-VAE model\n  - Synthetic data generator\n  - Training pipeline\n\n  Backend Engineer needs:\n  - Model input/output specifications\n  - Data shape and format\n\n  DevOps Lead needs:\n  - Python dependencies\n  - Memory/compute requirements\n\n  Architect reviews:\n  - Model architecture\n  - Data schema\n  - Integration points\n\ndependencies:\n  - ML provides: Model input/output specs\n  - Backend provides: Schema design\n  - DevOps provides: Compute requirements\n\nhandover_points:\n  day_2:\n    from: \"ml-engineer\"\n    to: \"backend-engineer\"\n    content: \"Model input/output specs\"\n\n  day_3:\n    from: \"backend-engineer\"\n    to: \"devops-lead\"\n    content: \"Python dependencies list\"\n\n  day_4:\n    from: \"all\"\n    to: \"architect\"\n    content: \"Design review and ADRs\"\n</agent_context>\n\n<context_from_previous_agents>\n=== backend-engineer output ===\n# Backend Engineering Code Review: FastAPI Ingestion & Inference Layers\n\nThis document presents a comprehensive review of the FastAPI route logic in [inference.py](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/inference.py) and [upload.py](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/upload.py). The review evaluates these routes against robustness, error handling, performance benchmarks, memory leak hazards, and architectural alignment as defined in the Week 2 Backend Guidelines and project constraints.\n\n---\n\n## 🛑 Blockers (Immediate Action Required)\n\n### 1. Tight-Coupling of ML Post-Processing inside FastAPI Routes\n\nIn [inference.py:L50-51](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/inference.py#L50-L51) and [L156-157](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/inference.py#L156-L157), VAE model raw reconstruction error scaling and clipping are implemented directly within the routing layer:\n\n```python\nraw_scores = detector.predict_batch(...)\nbatch_scores = np.clip(raw_scores / 2.0, 0.0, 1.0)\n```\n\n- **The Issue:** The dividing divisor (`2.0`) and clipping range (`0.0` to `1.0`) are mathematical post-processing heuristics. Hardcoding them in the API tier violates the **Loose Coupling** guardrail. If model configuration parameters change (such as hidden dimension scales or the beta divergence ratio), the reconstruction scores scale shifts, and the backend routes must be modified to prevent incorrect scaling or clipping.\n- **Consequence:** Structural coupling breaks modularity between the ML model and the delivery API.\n- **📝 Recommendation:** Move this scoring heuristic inside `AnomalyDetector.predict_batch` or a model post-processor class so the route layer only deals with finalized, scaled `[0..1]` scores.\n\n### 2. Unbounded Memory Leak in Model Caching (`_model_cache`)\n\nIn [inference.py:L12](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/models/inference.py#L12) and [L35-46](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/models/inference.py#L35-L46), loaded VAE models are cached indefinitely in a class-level dictionary:\n\n```python\n_model_cache: Dict[str, LSTMVAE] = {}\n```\n\n- **The Issue:** There is no cache eviction strategy (like Least-Recently-Used or TTL pruning). Over time, in a multi-tenant setup where dynamically-trained tenant-specific models (checkpoints) are reloaded across hundreds or thousands of clients, this cache will grow unbounded.\n- **Consequence:** This will cause a progressive memory leak, eventually triggering server-wide container Out Of Memory (OOM) fatal crashes.\n- **📝 Recommendation:** Integrate `cachetools.LRUCache` or a simple queue-based size constraint (e.g., maximum 10 models in memory, evicting the oldest) inside `AnomalyDetector`'s model loader.\n\n### 3. Blind Temporal Index Matching & Race Condition in Score Persist\n\nIn [inference.py:L58-76](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/inference.py#L58-L76), anomaly scores are matched and persisted back to the database purely based on sequence lists and index limits:\n\n```python\nstmt = select(SensorReading).where(\n    SensorReading.tenant_id == request.tenant_id,\n    SensorReading.greenhouse_id == request.greenhouse_id,\n).order_by(SensorReading.timestamp.desc()).limit(len(batch_scores))\n...\nfor idx, record in enumerate(reversed(records)):\n    if idx < len(batch_scores):\n        record.anomaly_score = float(batch_scores[idx])\n```\n\n- **The Issue:** Timestamps or unique reading identifiers of the incoming sequence array are not compared against the database records! The code assumes the latest database entries perfectly correspond to the array elements.\n- **Consequence:**\n  1. If a client transmits historical data or out-of-order sequences, wrong anomaly scores will be written to random database rows.\n  2. If the DB contains fewer records than the request batch size ($K < \\text{len}(scores)$), the database update fails silently for the trailing scores.\n  3. Under concurrent uploads, standard race conditions will result in database entries receiving completely mismatched scores.\n- **📝 Recommendation:** Expose specific record IDs or explicit timestamps in the `InferenceRequest` body, and utilize a targeted bulk update query with exact matches instead of sliding index assumptions.\n\n### 4. Excessive In-Memory Allocations during CSV Upload (Scale Memory Leak)\n\nIn [upload.py:L25-27](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/upload.py#L25-L27) and [inference.py:L110](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/inference.py#L110), file contents are read entirely into memory as raw bytes, decoded to unicode strings, and loaded as full Pandas DataFrames:\n\n```python\ncontent = await file.read()\ndf = pd.read_csv(StringIO(content.decode(\"utf-8\")))\n```\n\n- **The Issue:** Reading large uploaded bulk files directly into RAM creates a triple-copy allocation peak (raw bytes + decoded unicode string + String IO buffers + parsed DataFrame objects).\n- **Consequence:** If a vendor attempts a bulk historics upload of 50M-100MB, the server RAM usage spikes by 500M-1GB, causing massive latency bottlenecks or OOM aborts.\n- **📝 Recommendation:** Stream the file chunks to a temporary filesystem path (`tempfile`), or process the stream in batches using pandas' `chunksize` parameters. Ensure file unlinking in `inference.py` is safely wrapped in `try...finally` boundaries.\n\n### 5. RAM Spill via Thick SQLAlchemy ORM Object Instantiation\n\nIn [upload.py:L58-71](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/upload.py#L58-L71), bulk CSV records are iterated, individual Python objects are initialized, and tracked via session:\n\n```python\nrecords = []\nfor _, row in df.iterrows():\n    reading = SensorReading(...)\n    records.append(reading)\ndb.add_all(records)\n```\n\n- **The Issue:** Generating individual thick SQLAlchemy ORM instances for thousands of CSV readings forces the AsyncSession identity map to maintain full state tracking, dirty-checks, and session reference markers for thousands of Python objects.\n- **Consequence:** Memory spills inside the active thread and bulk inserts are exceptionally sluggish.\n- **📝 Recommendation:** Utilize standard SQLAlchemy 2.0 Core bulk inserts to completely bypass ORM state tracking overhead:\n  ```python\n  from sqlalchemy import insert\n  # Prepare a fast payload list of raw dicts\n  records_dict = df.to_dict(orient=\"records\")\n  await db.execute(insert(SensorReading), records_dict)\n  ```\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Warnings (Areas to Improve)\n\n### 1. Vendor Abstraction Layer Bypass & Severe Code Duplication\n\n- **The Issue:** The specialized [CSVAdapter](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/vendors/csv_adapter.py) engineering in Week 2 was specifically designed to handle CSV uploads, parse inputs, standardise column sets, validate types, and convert timezone-naive timestamps to timezone-aware UTC format. Yet, [upload.py](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/upload.py) completely bypasses `CSVAdapter`, manually duplicating Pandas CSV parsers, checking column subsets, and validating limits directly in the route file.\n- **Consequence:** Violates modular separation of concerns and creates duplicated parsing points. If the csv model schema layout changes, both the adapter and the endpoint routes must be manually updated.\n- **📝 Recommendation:** Refactor `/upload` in `upload.py` to ingest and process data directly through `CSVAdapter`.\n\n### 2. Timezone Loss on Insertion (Data Inconsistencies)\n\n- **The Issue:** The physical database column `SensorReading.timestamp` is designated as `DateTime(timezone=True)`. However, `/upload` parses dates with `pd.to_datetime(df['timestamp'])` which strips timezone markers, producing timezone-naive timestamps.\n- **Consequence:** SQLite silently accepts raw timezone-naive inserts. Over time, when queries convert readings or filter them, timezone discrepancies skew historical graphs by hours.\n- **📝 Recommendation:** Enforce UTC conversion. Relying on `CSVAdapter.get_data()` automatically enforces and converts all timestamps into pristine timezone-aware UTC structures.\n\n### 3. Hardcoded Physical Boundary Thresholds\n\n- **The Issue:** Physical limit bounds of numerical variables in `/upload` lines 40-47 are hard-coded directly as raw numeric literals `(0, 50)`, `(0, 100)`, `(0, 1000)`, `(0, 2000)` inside the route.\n- **Consequence:** This violates backend development instructions: _\"Hard-coded thresholds should be in src/config.py — WARN\"_.\n- **📝 Recommendation:** Centralize physical canonical boundaries into `src/config.py` as globally shared constants, importing them into schemas and validators alike, as follows:\n  ```python\n  CANONICAL_TEMP_LIMITS = (0.0, 50.0)\n  CANONICAL_HUMID_LIMITS = (0.0, 100.0)\n  CANONICAL_CO2_LIMITS = (0.0, 1000.0)\n  CANONICAL_LIGHT_LIMITS = (0.0, 2000.0)\n  ```\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Quality, Schema & Documentation Pass Audit\n\n- **Type Hints:** Both modules contain full python type hinting across functions, return payloads, and FastAPI dependency annotations (`AsyncSession = Depends(get_db)`).\n- **Docstrings:** High quality and complete function descriptors detailing input and schema specifications.\n- **Pydantic Validation (Ingress Layer):** The API schemas are correctly authored in **Pydantic v2** conventions. High-quality tensor validation rules in `InferenceRequest` successfully block mismatched matrices (`seq_len != 20` or `input_dim != 4`) early before reaching PyTorch space.\n- **DB Guardrail Compliance:** All schema operations query and filter by the mandatory multi-tenant field `tenant_id` consistently, aligning with strict security requirements.\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 Recommended Action Plan\n\n### Step 1: Encapsulate Scoring Scale to Resolve Tight-Coupling\n\nModify `AnomalyDetector.predict` or `predict_batch` inside [src/models/inference.py](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/models/inference.py) to integrate raw scaling and clipping internally:\n\n```python\n# Inside AnomalyDetector:\ndef predict_batch(self, sequences: List[np.ndarray]) -> np.ndarray:\n    stacked = np.stack(sequences)\n    raw_scores = self.predict(stacked)\n    # Scaled and clipped post-processing encapsulated safely inside the model wrapper\n    return np.clip(raw_scores / 2.0, 0.0, 1.0)\n```\n\nThis cleans up [inference.py:L51](file:///Users/eugenesidorov/Projects/ai-agronomist/src/api/routes/inference.py#L51) and avoids machine learning heuristic exposure in the routing layer.\n\n### Step 2: Implement LRU Eviction in Model Cache\n\nImport and utilize `cachetools` or restrict size dynamically:\n\n```python\n# Inside AnomalyDetector:\ndef _load_model(self) -> LSTMVAE:\n    if len(self._model_cache) > 10:\n        # Evict oldest loaded model to limit RAM footprint\n        first_key = next(iter(self._model_cache))\n        self._model_cache.pop(first_key)\n    ...\n```\n\n### Step 3: Refactor CSV Upload route to use `CSVAdapter` and SQLAlchemy Core Bulk Ingest\n\nReplace manual pandas operations in `upload.py` with the robust `CSVAdapter` workflow:\n\n```python\n# Refactored upload.py snippet:\n@router.post(\"/upload\", response_model=UploadResponse)\nasync def upload_csv(\n    file: UploadFile = File(...),\n    tenant_id: str = Form(...),\n    greenhouse_id: str = Form(\"default\"),\n    db: AsyncSession = Depends(get_db)\n):\n    if not file.filename.endswith('.csv'):\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=400, detail=\"Only CSV files are supported\")\n\n    content = await file.read()\n\n    # Process through the specialized CSV Adapter safely\n    with tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile(delete=False, suffix=\".csv\") as tmp:\n        tmp.write(content)\n        tmp_path = tmp.name\n\n    try:\n        adapter = CSVAdapter({\"filepath\": tmp_path})\n        df = adapter.get_data() # Validates, UTC converts, and converts schemas cleanly!\n    except Exception as e:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=422, detail=f\"Data pipeline validation failed: {str(e)}\")\n    finally:\n        if os.path.exists(tmp_path):\n            os.unlink(tmp_path)\n\n    # Core bulk insert to bypass individual ORM object overhead\n    records_dict = []\n    for _, row in df.iterrows():\n        records_dict.append({\n            \"tenant_id\": tenant_id,\n            \"greenhouse_id\": greenhouse_id,\n            \"timestamp\": row['timestamp'].to_pydatetime(),\n            \"temperature\": row['temperature'],\n            \"humidity\": row['humidity'],\n            \"co2\": row['co2'],\n            \"light\": row['light']\n        })\n\n    from sqlalchemy import insert\n    try:\n        await db.execute(insert(SensorReading), records_dict)\n        await db.commit()\n    except Exception as e:\n        await db.rollback()\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=500, detail=f\"Database persistent error: {str(e)}\")\n```\n\n\n=== ml-engineer output ===\n# ML Code Review: `run_inference_from_csv`\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n\nThis review covers the machine learning preprocessing and inference logic within the `run_inference_from_csv` function in `src/api/routes/inference.py`. The function is responsible for parsing a user-uploaded CSV, preparing the data for our LSTM-VAE model, running anomaly detection, and returning the results.\n\nOverall, the implementation is a solid first version, but there are several critical areas that require improvement to ensure robustness, maintainability, and model performance. The most significant issues are the use of hardcoded magic numbers for score scaling and anomaly thresholding, which violate project guardrails and make the system brittle.\n\n## 2. Key Findings & Recommendations\n\n### 🛑 Blocker: Hardcoded Magic Numbers\n\n**Finding:** The code contains two hardcoded magic numbers that are critical to the ML logic:\n\n1.  `batch_scores = np.clip(raw_scores / 2.0, 0.0, 1.0)`: The score normalization divisor `2.0` is arbitrary and not based on any statistical property of the model's output. This can lead to incorrectly scaled scores if the reconstruction error distribution changes.\n2.  `anomalies_detected = int((batch_scores > 0.8).sum())`: The anomaly threshold `0.8` is hardcoded. This is a critical hyperparameter that should be configurable and tuned on a validation dataset.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n\n- **[BLOCKER]** Both the normalization divisor and the anomaly threshold _must_ be moved to `src/config.py`.\n- The normalization divisor should be replaced with a more robust scaling method. I recommend using the 99th percentile of the reconstruction error from the validation set, which should be stored as part of the model's metadata.\n- The anomaly threshold should be determined systematically (e.g., using F1-score optimization on a labeled validation set) and stored in `src/config.py`.\n\n### ⚠️ Warning: Inefficient Data Handling\n\n**Finding:** The current implementation reads the entire CSV file into memory and then writes it to a temporary file. This approach is not scalable and will fail with large files due to excessive memory consumption.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n\n- **[MEDIUM]** Refactor the data loading logic to use `pandas.read_csv` with the `chunksize` parameter. This will allow for processing the data in a memory-efficient streaming manner.\n\n### 📝 Recommendation: Improved Timestamp Mapping\n\n**Finding:** The logic for mapping anomaly scores back to timestamps (`row_idx = idx + seq_len - 1`) assumes the data is a continuous, sorted time series. This could lead to incorrect timestamp associations if the CSV contains gaps or is unsorted.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n\n- **[LOW]** Add a validation step to ensure the `timestamp` column is sorted before processing. If it's not sorted, either sort it or raise an informative error to the user. This will make the timestamp mapping more reliable.\n\n### 📝 Recommendation: Add Structured Logging\n\n**Finding:** The function lacks structured logging, which is essential for debugging and monitoring in a production environment.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n\n- **[LOW]** Integrate a logging library (e.g., `loguru` or the standard `logging` module) to record key events, such as:\n  - The number of rows and sequences processed.\n  - The model ID and checkpoint path used for inference.\n  - The number of anomalies detected.\n  - The execution time for different stages (data prep, inference).\n\n## 3. Code Review Checklist\n\n| Category       | Status                       | Comments                                                                                                  |\n| -------------- | ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Quality**    | ⚠️ **Warning**               | Code is clean, but hardcoded values violate the \"no magic numbers\" principle.                             |\n| **Testing**    | ❓ **Unknown**               | No unit tests were provided for this function. Tests are needed to verify the preprocessing logic.        |\n| **Docs**       | ✅ **Passes**                | The function has a clear docstring.                                                                       |\n| **Arch**       | ✅ **Passes**                | The function is well-structured and follows a clear separation of concerns.                               |\n| **Guardrails** | 🛑 **VIOLATION (STOP WORK)** | Hardcoded sensor ranges and ML-specific constants (threshold, scaling factor) violate project guardrails. |\n\n\n=== tech-lead output ===\n# Frontend Review: `src/dashboard/pages/5_upload_operations.py`\n\n## 1. User Experience (UX)\n\n### Positive Aspects\n\n- **Clear Layout**: The use of two tabs (`Upload & Train Model` and `Upload & Find Anomalies`) provides a clear and intuitive separation between the two primary user workflows.\n- **Good Feedback**: The UI provides good feedback during operations, using `st.spinner` to indicate processing and `st.success`, `st.error`, and `st.warning` messages to inform the user of the outcome.\n- **Interactive Visualizations**: The anomaly detection tab includes a Plotly chart, which is a great way to visualize the results and make them easier for the user to understand.\n\n### Recommendations for Improvement\n\n- **Input Validation**: The `tenant_id` and `greenhouse_id` text inputs lack validation. A user could potentially submit empty or invalid values, which might lead to backend errors.\n  - **Suggestion**: Implement basic client-side validation to ensure that these fields are not empty before the form is submitted.\n- **Hardcoded Anomaly Threshold**: The anomaly score chart has a hardcoded threshold of `0.8`. This value should ideally be fetched from the backend or a configuration file, as it might change.\n  - **Suggestion**: Retrieve the anomaly threshold from the API and use it to draw the threshold line on the chart.\n- **File Upload Constraints**: There are no constraints or feedback on the size or format of the uploaded CSV file. A very large file could cause the application to hang or crash.\n  - **Suggestion**: Add guidance on the expected CSV format and a reasonable file size limit.\n\n## 2. Structural Stability\n\n### Positive Aspects\n\n- **Readability**: The code is well-structured and easy to follow. The logic for each tab is self-contained.\n- **Error Handling**: The code includes `try...except` blocks to handle `requests.exceptions.RequestException`, which is good for catching API-related errors.\n\n### Recommendations for Improvement\n\n- **Graceful Error Handling**: The error messages, while present, could be more user-friendly. Displaying a raw exception message might not be very helpful to a non-technical user.\n  - **Suggestion**: Parse the error responses from the API and display a more meaningful error message to the user.\n- **Large File Uploads**: The current implementation reads the entire uploaded file into memory (`uploaded_file_train.getvalue()`). This can be a problem for very large files.\n  - **Suggestion**: For a more robust solution, consider streaming the file upload to the backend.\n\n## 3. API Integrations\n\n### Positive Aspects\n\n- **Clear API Endpoints**: The API endpoints being used are clearly defined and easy to understand.\n\n### Recommendations for Improvement\n\n- **Synchronous Training Request**: The training process is triggered by a single, synchronous `requests.post` call. For a long-running process like model training, this is not ideal. The request could time out, and the user has no way of knowing the status of the training process once it has started.\n  - **Suggestion**: The backend should expose an asynchronous endpoint for training. The frontend would initiate the training process and then poll a separate endpoint for status updates. This would provide a much better user experience.\n- **API URL Configuration**: The `API_URL` is imported from `src/config.py`. This is good, but for a production application, it would be better to have this configurable via environment variables.\n\n## Summary of Key Recommendations\n\n1. **Implement input validation** for `tenant_id` and `greenhouse_id`.\n2. **Move the hardcoded anomaly threshold** to a configurable value.\n3. **Implement an asynchronous training process** with status polling.\n4. **Improve error handling** to provide more user-friendly messages.\n5. **Consider streaming for large file uploads**.\n\n</context_from_previous_agents>\n\nTASK: Consolidate the findings from the backend-engineer, ml-engineer, and tech-lead into a final executive summary. Read the review documents (`be_review.md`, `ml_review.md`, `fe_review.md`) and synthesize them into a single, coherent report. The final report should provide a high-level overview of the issues and recommendations from all reviews.\n\nIMPORTANT: Write your complete output directly to the file: final_review_report.md\nUse the Write/Create file tools to write the content itself, not Python code that creates it."}}
