ry issues, especially since it's a class variable dictionary. To manage this I'll be exploring using `collections.OrderedDict` to implement the LRU cache manually, or using the `lru_cache` decorator.\n\n\n","textDelta":"","thinkingDelta":"**Implementing Cache Strategies**\n\nI'm now exploring how to best manage the `_model_cache` within the `AnomalyDetector` class. Using `functools.lru_cache` for methods and class instances reveals an interesting nuance; instance-level caching is used with methods but class/static methods and module-level functions cache globally. I'm considering limiting the `_model_cache` to 16 models to avoid memory issues, especially since it's a class variable dictionary. To manage this I'll be exploring using `collections.OrderedDict` to implement the LRU cache manually, or using the `lru_cache` decorator.\n\n\n","text":""}}
2026-06-09 14:59:31,727 - root - INFO - RAW WS MSG: {"seqNum":"76","timestampMicros":"1781013571726354","stepUpdate":{"cascadeId":"ca8353af85119ca62e016b227447bbe8","trajectoryId":"ca8353af85119ca62e016b227447bbe8","stepIndex":23,"state":"STATE_ACTIVE","source":"SOURCE_MODEL","target":"TARGET_USER","thinking":"**Refining the Model Cache**\n\nI've discovered the `AnomalyDetector` class includes a `_model_cache` dictionary, which is promising. However, the cache is unbounded, meaning it'll grow indefinitely with different customer models, which is concerning. The cache currently lacks controls to manage its size or implement an eviction policy, making it unsuitable for production. That must be fixed!\n\n\n**Considering Cache Location**\n\nI'm now weighing the best place to introduce the LRU cache. Implementing it directly in the `inference.py` routes, perhaps using `@lru_cache`, seems more straightforward than modifying the `AnomalyDetector` class itself. I've drafted a function to test that idea: a simple `get_anomaly_detector` with the `lru_cache` decorator.\n\n\n**Evaluating Internal Cache Strategy**\n\nI'm now focusing on leveraging `@lru_cache` in `get_anomaly_detector` within `inference.py`. This approach offers a clean solution for caching up to 16 `AnomalyDetector` instances. I'm exploring whether to also refine the internal `_model_cache` of the `AnomalyDetector` class itself, as well, for situations outside inference routes to mitigate potential leaks. I'm testing `functools.lru_cache` for a dictionary-level or class-level cache to provide a robust solution.\n\n\n**Implementing Cache Strategies**\n\nI'm now exploring how to best manage the `_model_cache` within the `AnomalyDetector` class. Using `functools.lru_cache` for methods and class instances reveals an interesting nuance; instance-level caching is used with methods but class/static methods and module-level functions cache globally. I'm considering limiting the `_model_cache` to 16 models to avoid memory issues, especially since it's a class variable dictionary. To manage this I'll be exploring using `collections.OrderedDict` to implement the LRU cache manually, or using the `lru_cache` decorator.\n\n\n**Caching PyTorch Models**\n\nI've decided to see if PyTorch models can be LRU cached via a helper function. In `src/models/inference.py`, I've implemented a `load_cached_model` function using `@lru_cache`, which loads a PyTorch model from a checkpoint. This function is now used within the `AnomalyDetector._load_model()` method to retrieve models from the cache when required.\n\n\n","textDelta":"","thinkingDelta":"**Caching PyTorch Models**\n\nI've decided to see if PyTorch models can be LRU cached via a helper function. In `src/models/inference.py`, I've implemented a `load_cached_model` function using `@lru_cache`, which loads a PyTorch model from a checkpoint. This function is now used within the `AnomalyDetector._load_model()` method to retrieve models from the cache when required.\n\n\n","text":""}}
