ry issues, especially since it's a class variable dictionary. To manage this I'll be exploring using `collections.OrderedDict` to implement the LRU cache manually, or using the `lru_cache` decorator.\n\n\n**Caching PyTorch Models**\n\nI've decided to see if PyTorch models can be LRU cached via a helper function. In `src/models/inference.py`, I've implemented a `load_cached_model` function using `@lru_cache`, which loads a PyTorch model from a checkpoint. This function is now used within the `AnomalyDetector._load_model()` method to retrieve models from the cache when required.\n\n\n**Validating Design Choices**\n\nI'm incredibly pleased with the current structure. Replacing the unbounded `_model_cache` with a bounded, thread-safe `functools.lru_cache` of size 16 feels elegant. I confirmed that caching at the `get_anomaly_detector` level in `src/api/routes/inference.py` prevents redundant `AnomalyDetector` instantiation with each API call, as well. Now I'm carefully double-checking the project requirements, and guardrails, to ensure compliance before I make changes.\n\n\n**Confirming Route-Level Cache**\n\nI'm now completely confident in caching within the inference routes. Defining `get_cached_detector` in `src/api/routes/inference.py` ensures compliance with constraints and perfectly addresses the memory leak problem. I'll use `@lru_cache` for a straightforward and effective solution, while avoiding touching other files.\n\n\n**Validating Hashability of Parameters**\n\nI've confirmed that `checkpoint_path`, used as the key for caching, is hashable, which is crucial for `@lru_cache` to work. I've updated the `run_inference` and `run_inference_from_csv` functions to utilize this cache and handle potential exceptions during model initialization. I'm now verifying that there are no remaining API endpoints in `src/api/routes/inference.py` that would require similar adjustments.\n\n\n**Reviewing All Inference Routes**\n\nI've carefully re-read lines 95-186 of `src/api/routes/inference.py` to ensure I correctly replace the existing code. I confirmed that the `/api/inference/upload` route will also be updated to use the cache. This completes the route-level cache integration and addresses all the identified memory leak issues.\n\n\n**Refactoring CSV Upload**\n\nI'm now refactoring the `/api/inference/upload` route, transitioning from loading the entire CSV into memory to a chunked, streaming approach using `await file.read(1024 * 1024)`. I've confirmed that this method works correctly with FastAPI's `UploadFile` class, returning empty bytes at the end of the file. I'm aiming to enhance both efficiency and memory management during CSV uploads.\n\n\n**Integrating Model Resolution**\n\nI'm now integrating active model resolution before CSV processing in the upload route. I've moved the `get_cached_detector` call and active model lookup before processing the CSV. This prevents unnecessary processing if the model isn't available and adds better error handling, immediately removing the temporary file if model loading fails.\n\n\n**Implementing Chunked CSV Processing**\n\nI'm now implementing the core loop for chunked CSV processing within the upload route. I'm using Pandas for chunked reading, and an `adapter` for normalizing the data within chunks. I'm considering chunk sizes, sequence length, and overlap to ensure correct operation. The process includes sequence windowing and anomaly detection within a `try...except` block, for handling CSV errors properly.\n\n\n**Finalizing CSV Processing Logic**\n\nI'm now completing the CSV chunk processing. The core logic involves combining current chunk data with overlap from the previous one. I'm dynamically updating the `prev_overlap_df`, and making sure I'm taking the sequence length into account for proper windowing and anomaly detection. I'm handling validations and errors robustly.\n\n\n**Finalizing CSV Processing Logic**\n\nI'm now completing the CSV chunk processing and focusing on handling 